# Response headers for Cloudflare Pages. Copied verbatim into dist/ by Astro;
# scripts/gen-csp.mjs then appends the Content-Security-Policy to the /* block
# after the build, because the CSP's script hashes only exist once dist/ does.
#
# Cloudflare semantics that shape this file:
# - Every rule whose path matches a request applies, and a header set by two
#   matching rules is JOINED with a comma, not overridden. So no header is set in
#   both /* and a narrower rule: Cache-Control lives only in the narrow rules,
#   and the security headers only in /*.
# - `! Header-Name` in a narrower rule detaches a header set by a broader one.
#   Nothing here needs it yet.
# - At most 100 rules, and a header line at most 2,000 characters.
#   scripts/check-build.mjs fails the build on either.
# - Comments stay at column 0, above a rule, never between its header lines.
#
# /* — every response:
# - HSTS for a year, subdomains included. No `preload`: preload is a commitment
#   to the browser vendors' hardcoded list that takes months to undo, worth making
#   only once every subdomain of rowshape.com is known to serve HTTPS.
# - Permissions-Policy: a docs site uses none of these. Denying them means an
#   injected script or a compromised dependency cannot prompt a visitor for them
#   under our origin.
# - X-Frame-Options is superseded by the CSP's frame-ancestors 'none' in every
#   current browser; kept for the ones that predate it.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# Vite names everything here after a hash of its content (and Expressive Code's
# ec.*.css/js after its own 5-character one), so a changed file is a new URL and
# a URL never changes meaning. That is the only condition under which `immutable`
# is safe; check-build fails if any file here lacks a hash in its name.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Pagefind's file names are only partly hashed: fragment/, index/ and the
# .pf_meta file are, but pagefind.js, pagefind-worker.js, the wasm.*.pagefind
# files and the UI bundles are not (pagefind-entry.json is fetched with a ?ts=
# cache-buster, so it is always fresh). The unhashed JS and the wasm must match
# each other and the index they read; `immutable` on them would pin a returning
# visitor to last deploy's search runtime against this deploy's index. Five
# minutes caps that window; the hashed files revalidate with a cheap 304, which
# costs little for a search that loads once per visit.
/pagefind/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
